[{:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-338513"] :question/problem "Consider the following boundary value problem.\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+4y(x)=0,\\,y(0)=y(1)=0,\\,0\\le x\\le 1\n$$\n\nWhich of the following best classifies the boundary conditions for this problem?" :question/worked-solution "For a second-order boundary value problem, \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+P(x){y}^{′}(x)+Q(x)y(x)=f(x),\\,a\\le x\\le b\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x),Q(x)$ and $f(x)$ are all continuous on $[a,b],$ we have the following boundary condition classifications. \n\n- Dirichlet:\n\n$$\ny(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,y(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\n- Neumann:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,{y}^{′}(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\n- Robin (mixed):\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}y(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}^{′}(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,{\\alpha }_{2}y(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}^{′}(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\n- Periodic:\n\n$$\ny(a)=y(b),\\,{y}^{′}(a)={y}^{′}(b)\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\gamma }_{i},{\\alpha }_{i},$ and ${\\beta }_{i}$ are real numbers with $({\\alpha }_{i},{\\beta }_{i})\\ne (0,0).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following boundary conditions, defined on $x=a=0$ and $x=b=1.$\n\n$$\ny(0)=0,\\,y(1)=0\n$$\n\nThese are Dirichlet boundary conditions." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186149821 :answer-field/correct 17592186149822 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186149827 :answer-field/correct 17592186149828 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186149818 :answer-field/correct 17592186149820 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186149824 :answer-field/correct 17592186149825 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-338516"] :question/problem "Consider the following boundary value problem (BVP).\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+{x}^{2}{y}^{′}(x)+y(x)=2x,\\,0\\le x\\le \\pi \n$$\n\n$$\n2y(0)+{y}^{′}(0)=0,\\,y(\\pi )+2{y}^{′}(\\pi )=0\n$$\n\nComplete the missing information below:\n\n$\\,$ The governing differential equation is {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ For this BVP, the {{field-2}} boundary conditions are {{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ The boundary value problem is {{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "For a second-order boundary value problem, \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+P(x){y}^{′}(x)+Q(x)y(x)=f(x),\\,a\\le x\\le b\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x),Q(x)$ and $f(x)$ are all continuous on $[a,b],$ we have the following boundary condition classifications. \n\n- Dirichlet:\n\n$$\ny(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,y(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\n- Neumann:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,{y}^{′}(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\n- Robin (mixed):\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}y(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}^{′}(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,{\\alpha }_{2}y(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}^{′}(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\gamma }_{i},{\\alpha }_{i},$ and ${\\beta }_{i}$ are real numbers with $({\\alpha }_{i},{\\beta }_{i})\\ne (0,0).$ \n\nA set of Dirichlet, Neumann, or Robin boundary conditions is homogeneous if both right-hand sides are zero (i.e., ${\\gamma }_{1}={\\gamma }_{2}=0.)$ Otherwise, they are inhomogeneous.\n\nA boundary value problem is homogeneous if the differential equation is homogeneous $(f≡0)$ and the corresponding boundary conditions are homogeneous. Otherwise, the BVP is inhomogeneous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement:\n\n- The governing differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+{x}^{2}{y}^{′}(x)+y(x)=2x.\n$$\n\nSince the right-hand side $f(x)=2x≢0,$ the equation is $\\text{inhomogeneous}.$\n\n- The boundary conditions are\n\n$$\n2y(0)+{y}^{′}(0)=0,\\,y(\\pi )+2{y}^{′}(\\pi )=0.\n$$\n\nThese are $\\text{Robin}$ boundary conditions, and since the right-hand sides are zero for both, they are $\\text{homogeneous}.$\n\n- Since the governing equation is inhomogeneous, yet the boundary conditions are homogeneous, the BVP is $\\text{inhomogeneous}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-338618/selection/ef6b3c8a066aa4f14722d27e35b31ab6414cf6cba0ded8a063a0b53e18174200" :answer/id #uuid "facf965b-3243-5a8d-8251-24b574016444" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Dirichlet" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338618/selection/b9248a6ddaa63032886818da7db1f1bcca7b4af333798c69a62e6a3c7eb9a71a" :answer/id #uuid "7eab5691-01e3-50b8-9d2b-2ed48d1f8747" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Robin" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338618/selection/eb17b3e164dea3327acae3b877cd3aefdf4b9450fb5eb47b0f9c4fa4f660223e" :answer/id #uuid "e0064102-c08e-588e-a35d-d5f5d01932c9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Periodic" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338618/selection/06285049f72ef30fee6ef6292e1826eabf19578fd3f3da4353e2d47878d19d9f" :answer/id #uuid "632ad8cf-b54f-55aa-a1aa-186d3ca82abd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Other" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338618/selection/c8a139a7781cbf1484c5c65d833041ae69c859d2c77c229a5e0cb871eee8b46b" :answer/id #uuid "66dfed4b-8930-534e-b251-7a9023ac8f1d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Neumann" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338618/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "7623e14c-5206-5bd0-a5da-344350db9827" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-338618/selection/b9248a6ddaa63032886818da7db1f1bcca7b4af333798c69a62e6a3c7eb9a71a" :answer-field/choices ["q-338618/selection/ef6b3c8a066aa4f14722d27e35b31ab6414cf6cba0ded8a063a0b53e18174200" "q-338618/selection/b9248a6ddaa63032886818da7db1f1bcca7b4af333798c69a62e6a3c7eb9a71a" "q-338618/selection/eb17b3e164dea3327acae3b877cd3aefdf4b9450fb5eb47b0f9c4fa4f660223e" "q-338618/selection/06285049f72ef30fee6ef6292e1826eabf19578fd3f3da4353e2d47878d19d9f" "q-338618/selection/c8a139a7781cbf1484c5c65d833041ae69c859d2c77c229a5e0cb871eee8b46b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-338618" :question/id #uuid "5b670b9b-dff6-597e-8aac-8d40070f8fbf" :question/math-academy-id "q-338618" :question/problem "Consider the following boundary value problem.\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+4y(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x),\\,2y(-2)-{y}^{′}(-2)=3,\\,y(1)+{y}^{′}(1)=-1,\\,-2\\le x\\le 1\n$$\n\nWhich of the following best classifies the boundary conditions for this problem?" :question/worked-solution "For a second-order boundary value problem, \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+P(x){y}^{′}(x)+Q(x)y(x)=f(x),\\,a\\le x\\le b\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x),Q(x)$ and $f(x)$ are all continuous on $[a,b],$ we have the following boundary condition classifications. \n\n- Dirichlet:\n\n$$\ny(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,y(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\n- Neumann:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,{y}^{′}(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\n- Robin (mixed):\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}y(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}^{′}(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,{\\alpha }_{2}y(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}^{′}(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\n- Periodic:\n\n$$\ny(a)=y(b),\\,{y}^{′}(a)={y}^{′}(b)\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\gamma }_{i},{\\alpha }_{i},$ and ${\\beta }_{i}$ are real numbers with $({\\alpha }_{i},{\\beta }_{i})\\ne (0,0).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following boundary conditions, defined on $x=a=-2$ and $x=b=1.$\n\n$$\n2y(-2)-{y}^{′}(-2)=3,\\,y(1)+{y}^{′}(1)=-1\n$$\n\nThese are Robin (mixed) boundary conditions." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-338618/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8c898fc-6802-5296-9866-c844f9ed0349"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-338618"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186149841 :answer-field/correct 17592186149843 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186149837 :answer-field/correct 17592186149838 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186149834 :answer-field/correct 17592186149836 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186149831 :answer-field/correct 17592186149832 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-338646"] :question/problem "Consider the following boundary value problem (BVP).\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x){y}^{′}(x)+3y(x)=0,\\,0\\le x\\le 2\n$$\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(0)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(2)=-1\n$$\n\nComplete the missing information below:\n\n$\\,$ The governing differential equation is {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ For this BVP, the {{field-2}} boundary conditions are {{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ The boundary value problem is {{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "For a second-order boundary value problem, \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+P(x){y}^{′}(x)+Q(x)y(x)=f(x),\\,a\\le x\\le b\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x),Q(x)$ and $f(x)$ are all continuous on $[a,b],$ we have the following boundary condition classifications. \n\n- Dirichlet:\n\n$$\ny(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,y(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\n- Neumann:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,{y}^{′}(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\n- Robin (mixed):\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}y(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}^{′}(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,{\\alpha }_{2}y(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}^{′}(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\gamma }_{i},{\\alpha }_{i},$ and ${\\beta }_{i}$ are real numbers with $({\\alpha }_{i},{\\beta }_{i})\\ne (0,0).$ \n\nA set of Dirichlet, Neumann, or Robin boundary conditions is homogeneous if both right-hand sides are zero (i.e., ${\\gamma }_{1}={\\gamma }_{2}=0.)$ Otherwise, they are inhomogeneous.\n\nA boundary value problem is homogeneous if the differential equation is homogeneous $(f≡0)$ and the corresponding boundary conditions are homogeneous. Otherwise, the BVP is inhomogeneous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement:\n\n- The governing differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x){y}^{′}(x)+3y(x)=0.\n$$\n\nSince the right-hand side $f(x)≡0,$ the equation is $\\text{homogeneous}.$\n\n- The boundary conditions are\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(0)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(2)=-1.\n$$\n\nThese are $\\text{Neumann}$ boundary conditions, and since the right-hand sides are not zero for both, they are $\\text{inhomogeneous}.$\n\n- Since the governing equation is homogeneous, yet the boundary conditions are inhomogeneous, the BVP is $\\text{inhomogeneous}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-338695/field-1/0dccd1c0d01f1a6108a63b9727608bbe91e40a553b6f3a4aeaca05919f28c3b7" :answer/id #uuid "0eea5460-9fce-5292-8a2c-a5d61a66b74d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338695/field-1/668dba9073658905cfbd208ef398f5d3f3cf0216b5f756007174dd24f51b21a9" :answer/id #uuid "71513aac-53cc-5e33-81cb-39b4047cef69" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338695/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "5893df9b-3f00-5fcc-a94f-3423a92dbaeb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-338695/field-1/0dccd1c0d01f1a6108a63b9727608bbe91e40a553b6f3a4aeaca05919f28c3b7" :answer-field/choices ["q-338695/field-1/0dccd1c0d01f1a6108a63b9727608bbe91e40a553b6f3a4aeaca05919f28c3b7" "q-338695/field-1/668dba9073658905cfbd208ef398f5d3f3cf0216b5f756007174dd24f51b21a9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-338695/field-2/b9248a6ddaa63032886818da7db1f1bcca7b4af333798c69a62e6a3c7eb9a71a" :answer/id #uuid "d34aa6b8-a20f-5a9f-a816-56d3681f98ce" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Robin" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338695/field-2/c8a139a7781cbf1484c5c65d833041ae69c859d2c77c229a5e0cb871eee8b46b" :answer/id #uuid "3a56026b-35fa-55a0-be5b-45979c6d3b30" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Neumann" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338695/field-2/ef6b3c8a066aa4f14722d27e35b31ab6414cf6cba0ded8a063a0b53e18174200" :answer/id #uuid "2469e65b-db6c-5ae7-889d-ec2871a9671e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Dirichlet" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338695/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "21c8ee33-e7a4-5857-a896-6cb023a28d9c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-338695/field-2/ef6b3c8a066aa4f14722d27e35b31ab6414cf6cba0ded8a063a0b53e18174200" :answer-field/choices ["q-338695/field-2